Done. `polyglot/main.py.c` (i.e. `/app/polyglot/main.py.c`) is in place and verified by an implementor sub-turn, since this role could only write under docs/specs/*.md.

How the polyglot works:
- Line 1 is `#if 0`, which Python reads as a comment and C treats as a dead block start, so the Python program (lines 2–22) is invisible to gcc.
- Line 23 is `"""`, which Python reads as the start of a string literal that runs until the closing `"""` on the last line (line 91) — swallowing the entire C program so Python never parses it.
- Line 24 `#endif` closes the dead block, and lines 26–90 are ordinary C.
- The final line `//"""` starts with `//`, so C reads it as a comment rather than an unterminated string.

Behaviour: Python uses native big integers; C uses a base-10⁹ bignum accumulator, so both are arbitrary-precision and agree for any N.

Verification run: gcc compiled cleanly (one expected warning from the bare `"""` line), and both routes printed matching values for N = 0, 1, 2, 3, 10, 50, 100 (0, 1, 1, 2, 55, 12586269025, 354224848179261915075). For N = 1000 the two outputs were byte-identical and matched an independent Python reference.

